proposed_statement_changes.proposed:
Put \(D=Sd\) and consider nonempty configurations, which necessarily satisfy \(B\ge D^2\).  For every positive \(N,T,n_1,\ldots,n_S\), every \(P\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}^{\dagger}(\lambda)\), and \(P\)-almost every admissible event state,
\[
P_P\{\tau_n(z_0)\in C_\alpha\mid E_0\}\ge1-\alpha.
\]
For fixed sample sizes, call an interval \(I\) finite-sample conditionally honest if it is observed-data measurable and satisfies this same inequality with \(I\) in place of \(C_\alpha\), uniformly over \(P\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}^{\dagger}(\lambda)\).  Along every array with \(N\wedge T\wedge\min_s n_s\to\infty\), the following conclusions hold.  For fixed interior-compatible envelope constants \(B>D^2\), there are constants \(0<c_\alpha<C_\alpha^{\mathrm{up}}<\infty\), with permitted dependence on the fixed slack \(B-D^2\), such that
\[
c_\alpha R_{\star,\beta}^{1/2}
\le
\inf_{I:\,\text{finite-sample conditionally honest}}
\sup_{P\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}^{\dagger}(\lambda)}
\mathbb E_P[\operatorname{length}(I)\mid E_0]
\le
\sup_{P\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}^{\dagger}(\lambda)}
\mathbb E_P[\operatorname{length}(C_\alpha)\mid E_0]
\le C_\alpha^{\mathrm{up}}R_{\star,\beta}^{1/2}.
\]
At the compatible boundary \(B=D^2\), exact coverage and the same upper bound remain valid, while the asserted lower bound is only
\[
c_\alpha
\{R_{\mathrm{tr}}\vee R_{\mathrm{sm}}\vee R_{\mathrm{sp},\beta}\}^{1/2}
\le
\inf_{I:\,\text{finite-sample conditionally honest}}
\sup_{P\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}^{\dagger}(\lambda)}
\mathbb E_P[\operatorname{length}(I)\mid E_0];
\]
no universal \(R_{\mathrm{don}}\) converse is asserted there.  Both lower bounds also hold for uniformly asymptotically conditionally honest interval sequences by an \(\epsilon\)-slack argument.  The historical and event concentration steps condition on the complete historical design, \(E_0\), and all donors; the donor-deviation probability is then integrated out conditional on \(E_0\), and the three failure probabilities are combined by Bonferroni.  The result requires neither temporal outcome mixing within a unit, identical unit response laws, studentization, a dense-local-site condition, nor sequential overlap.  The log-adjusted spacing term is uniform and unavoidable under design-only geometric beta mixing and the maintained marginal-density assumptions.  Configurations \(B<D^2\) are empty and receive no substantive minimax interpretation.

